      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: The council chamber fights back with **arcane traps, illusion effects, and environmental hazards**\u2014especially targeting anyone without amulets\u2014causing paralysis, force damage, disorientation, and falling debris.\n\nThe room itself actively interferes with combat in a few key ways:\n\n- **Arcane trap bursts (CANON/OBSERVED)**: These can hit players (especially if they lack amulets), dealing **2d6 force damage** and even **temporarily paralyzing** them. Guards are **exempt due to amulets**, creating an uneven battlefield.\n\n- **Targeting logic (CANON)**: The chamber\u2019s defenses specifically **target players without amulets**, meaning the environment \u201cchooses sides.\u201d\n\n- **Illusion effects (CANON)**: The party can become **disoriented by illusory walls/illusion spells**, and they\u2019ve already been **thrown off by illusory duplicates**, making positioning and targeting unreliable.\n\n- **Falling debris hazards (OBSERVED)**: The fight space itself becomes dangerous, forcing players to **dodge falling debris hazards** mid-combat.\n\n- **Rune alarms & sensory cues (OBSERVED/CANON)**: **Rune alarms** escalate the situation (bringing guards), and shifts in the **ambient hum** can signal something about to trigger\u2014adding pressure and unpredictability.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Defenses: Arcane traps (damage + paralysis), amulet-based targeting exemption  \n- Terrain Hazards: Falling debris  \n- Magical Effects: Illusions (walls, duplicates), disorientation  \n- Environmental Feedback: Rune alarms, changing wall-hum cues",
      "embedding_similarity": null
    },
    {
      "id": "q_the_emergency_council_meeting_2_v2",
